  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nWe will use a multi-resolution gradient descent approach to optimize the step function. This method dynamically adjusts the sequence length and optimization parameters to find the globally minimal objective value without relying on linear programming solvers. The core idea is to start from a given initial sequence, apply gradient-based optimization with multiple lengths, and then progressively refine the solution. We will use FFT-based convolution computations for efficiency and accuracy.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\nWe minimize objective J(a) = 2 * n * [max_k | (a * a)[k] |] / (sum(a))^2\nSubject to:\n   a_i >= 0 for all i\n   sum(a) >= 0.01 (handled internally)\n   a_i <= 1000 (handled internally)\nThe gradient descent approach iteratively optimizes the sequence coefficients using a smoothed exponential utility to escape local minima and dead zones.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\nWe'll implement the following steps:\n\n   a. If 'height_sequence_1' exists, initialize the sequence with it\n      else use a random sequence of length 800.\n\n   b. Use delayed-normalization: maintain some scale degrees of freedom\n      until the full convolution is properly scaled.\n\n   c. Apply gradients as a sequence of steps to refine the solution.\n\n   d. The search will iterate with tolerance decreases and trials.\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\nWe'll select from a set of optimization configurations:\n\n   - Initialization: Use either the parent sequence if available, or a random uniform sequence.\n   - Optimization Method: SmoothMaxGradientDescent with temperature decay.\n   - Coordinate Descent: Start from uniform and optimize with Laplacian perturbations.\n   - Multi-resolution: Sample sequences with length 800, 750, 700, 900, and 950.\n   - Early Termination Threshold: Set to 0.0001 after convergence or time limit.\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\nThe main phases will be allocated:\n\n   - Phase 1: Multi-resolution exploration (up to 300s)\n   - Phase 2: Fine-tuning (up to 450s)\n   - Phase 3: Peak equalization (up to 450s)\n\nEach phase uses internal clock management to avoid time overflow, including budget allocation based on real-time performance.\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\nFinal candidate will be:\n\n   - Checked for all finite, non-negative coefficients\n   - Convolution is computed exactly as required\n   - Resulting sequence is returned after checking with `evaluate_sequence`\n\nThe implementation uses strict tolerance enforcement (1e-13) to prevent floating-point issues and `np.clip` to ensure defensive bounds.\n\nLet me write the code accordingly.",
